  "all::Nov2025_4WM2H__Q20__p19-19__m05__Simultaneous-Equations": {
    "status": "checked",
    "checkedBy": "Dr Eslam Ahmed + Codex",
    "updated": "2026-05-16T09:02:20",
    "topicNote": "",
    "steps": [
      {
        "title": "Step 1 Substitute y (",
        "body": "**Step 1: Substitute *y* = (*x*+2) into the quadratic equation.**\n*x*<sup>2</sup> + (*x*+2)<sup>2</sup> = 3*x*(*x*+2) − 11"
      },
      {
        "title": "Step 2: Expand all brackets.",
        "body": "*x*<sup>2</sup> + (*x*<sup>2</sup>+4*x*+4) = 3*x*<sup>2</sup> + 6*x* − 11\n2*x*<sup>2</sup> + 4*x* + 4 = 3*x*<sup>2</sup> + 6*x* − 11"
      },
      {
        "title": "Step 3: Move all terms to one side to set the equation to zero.",
        "body": "0 = 3*x*<sup>2</sup> − 2*x*<sup>2</sup> + 6*x* − 4*x* − 11 − 4\n*x*<sup>2</sup> + 2*x* − 15 = 0"
      },
      {
        "title": "Step 4: Factorise the quadratic equation.",
        "body": "(*x*+5)(*x*−3) = 0\nSo, *x* =  − 5 or *x* = 3.\n\n**Step 5: Find corresponding *y* values using *y* = *x* + 2.**\n\nIf *x* =  − 5 ⟹ *y* =  − 5 + 2 =  − 3\n\nIf *x* = 3 ⟹ *y* = 3 + 2 = 5"
      }
    ],
    "finalAnswer": "(−5,−3) and (3,5)"
  },
  "all::Nov2025_4WM2H__Q21__p20-21__m07__Functions": {
    "status": "checked",
    "checkedBy": "Dr Eslam Ahmed + Codex",
    "updated": "2026-05-16T09:02:20",
    "topicNote": "",
    "steps": [
      {
        "title": "(a) Find g ( 1)",
        "body": "**(a) Find *g*(−1):**\n*g*(−1) = 4(−1)<sup>2</sup> + 16(−1) − 9 = 4 − 16 − 9 =  − 21"
      },
      {
        "title": "Answer",
        "body": "\\(a\\)  − 21\n\n**(b) Find *f**g*(*x*):**\n*f**g*(*x*) = *f*(*g*(*x*)) = 5(4*x*<sup>2</sup>+16*x*−9) − 3\n = 20*x*<sup>2</sup> + 80*x* − 45 − 3 = 20*x*<sup>2</sup> + 80*x* − 48"
      },
      {
        "title": "Answer",
        "body": "\\(b\\) 20*x*<sup>2</sup> + 80*x* − 48\n\nLet *y* = 4*x*<sup>2</sup> + 16*x* − 9. We must rearrange to make *x* the subject.\n\n**Step 1: Complete the square for the *x* terms.**\n*y* = 4(*x*<sup>2</sup>+4*x*) − 9\n*y* = 4((*x*+2)<sup>2</sup>−4) − 9\n*y* = 4(*x*+2)<sup>2</sup> − 16 − 9\n*y* = 4(*x*+2)<sup>2</sup> − 25\n\n**Step 2: Rearrange for *x*.**\n*y* + 25 = 4(*x*+2)<sup>2</sup>\n$$\\\\frac{y + 25}{4} = (x + 2)^2$$\n$$x + 2 = \\\\pm\\\\sqrt{\\\\frac{y + 25}{4}}$$\n\nSince *x* ≥  − 2, *x* + 2 is positive, so we take the positive root.\n$$x + 2 = \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{y + 25}}{2}$$\n$$x = \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{y + 25}}{2} - 2$$\n\n**Step 3: Swap *y* for *x* to write the inverse function.**"
      }
    ],
    "finalAnswer": "$g^{-1}(x) = \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{x + 25}}{2} - 2$\n\nWhen finding the inverse of a quadratic, always complete the square. The restricted domain (*x* ≥  − 2) tells you whether to take the positive or negative square root!"
  },
  "all::Nov2025_4WM2H__Q22__p22-22__m06__Coordinate-Geometry": {
    "status": "checked",
    "checkedBy": "Dr Eslam Ahmed + Codex",
    "updated": "2026-05-16T09:02:20",
    "topicNote": "",
    "steps": [
      {
        "title": "Step 1: Differentiate the curve to find the gradient expression.",
        "body": "$$\\\\frac{dy}{dx} = 3x^2 - 16$$"
      },
      {
        "title": "Step 2: Set the gradient to 11 to find the x-coordinates.",
        "body": "3*x*<sup>2</sup> − 16 = 11\n3*x*<sup>2</sup> = 27\n*x*<sup>2</sup> = 9 ⟹ *x* = 3 or *x* =  − 3"
      },
      {
        "title": "Step 3: Find the corresponding y-coordinates on the curve.",
        "body": "For *x* = 3: *y* = (3)<sup>3</sup> − 16(3) + 7 = 27 − 48 + 7 =  − 14"
      },
      {
        "title": "Point 1: (3,−14)",
        "body": "For *x* =  − 3: *y* = (−3)<sup>3</sup> − 16(−3) + 7 =  − 27 + 48 + 7 = 28"
      },
      {
        "title": "Point 2: (−3,28)",
        "body": "**Step 4: Find the y-intercept (*b*) for each tangent (*y* = 11*x* + *b*).**\n\nUsing (3,−14):\n − 14 = 11(3) + *b* ⟹  − 14 = 33 + *b* ⟹ *b* =  − 47\n\nUsing (−3,28):\n28 = 11(−3) + *b* ⟹ 28 =  − 33 + *b* ⟹ *b* = 61"
      }
    ],
    "finalAnswer": "*b* =  − 47 and 61"
  },
  "all::Nov2025_4WM2H__Q24__p24-25__m06__Vectors": {
    "status": "checked",
    "checkedBy": "Dr Eslam Ahmed + Codex",
    "updated": "2026-05-16T09:02:20",
    "topicNote": "",
    "steps": [
      {
        "title": "(a) Find $\\\\overrightarrow{AC}$",
        "body": "$$\\\\overrightarrow{AC} = \\\\overrightarrow{AO} + \\\\overrightarrow{OC} = -\\\\overrightarrow{OA} + \\\\overrightarrow{OC} = -5\\\\mathbf{a} + 10\\\\mathbf{b}$$"
      },
      {
        "title": "Answer",
        "body": "\\(a\\)  − 5**a** + 10**b**"
      },
      {
        "title": "(b) Find $\\\\overrightarrow{OB}$",
        "body": "$$\\\\overrightarrow{OB} = \\\\overrightarrow{OA} + \\\\overrightarrow{AB} = 5\\\\mathbf{a} + (2\\\\mathbf{a} + 4\\\\mathbf{b}) = 7\\\\mathbf{a} + 4\\\\mathbf{b}$$"
      },
      {
        "title": "Answer",
        "body": "\\(b\\) 7**a** + 4**b**\n\n**Step 1: Set up vector equations for $\\\\overrightarrow{OP}$.** Since *O**P**B* is a straight line, $\\\\overrightarrow{OP}$ is a scalar multiple of $\\\\overrightarrow{OB}$. Let this be *λ*.\n$$\\\\overrightarrow{OP} = \\\\lambda(7\\\\mathbf{a} + 4\\\\mathbf{b}) = 7\\\\lambda\\\\mathbf{a} + 4\\\\lambda\\\\mathbf{b}$$\n\nSince *P* lies on the straight line *A**C*, $\\\\overrightarrow{AP} = \\\\mu\\\\overrightarrow{AC}$ for some scalar *μ*.\n$$\\\\overrightarrow{OP} = \\\\overrightarrow{OA} + \\\\overrightarrow{AP} = 5\\\\mathbf{a} + \\\\mu(-5\\\\mathbf{a} + 10\\\\mathbf{b})$$\n$$\\\\overrightarrow{OP} = (5 - 5\\\\mu)\\\\mathbf{a} + 10\\\\mu\\\\mathbf{b}$$\n\n**Step 2: Equate the coefficients of **a** and **b**.**\n7*λ* = 5 − 5*μ*  — (1)\n$$4\\\\lambda = 10\\\\mu \\\\implies \\\\mu = \\\\frac{4}{10}\\\\lambda = 0.4\\\\lambda \\\\quad \\\\text{--- (2)}$$\n\n**Step 3: Solve for *λ*.** Substitute (2) into (1):\n7*λ* = 5 − 5(0.4*λ*)\n7*λ* = 5 − 2*λ*\n$$9\\\\lambda = 5 \\\\implies \\\\lambda = \\\\frac{5}{9}$$"
      },
      {
        "title": "Step 4: Find $\\\\overrightarrow{OP}$.",
        "body": "$$\\\\overrightarrow{OP} = \\\\frac{5}{9}(7\\\\mathbf{a} + 4\\\\mathbf{b}) = \\\\frac{35}{9}\\\\mathbf{a} + \\\\frac{20}{9}\\\\mathbf{b}$$"
      }
    ],
    "finalAnswer": "$\\\\frac{35}{9}\\\\mathbf{a} + \\\\frac{20}{9}\\\\mathbf{b}$"
  },
  "all::Nov2025_4WM2H__Q25__p26-26__m03__Algebra-Toolkit": {
    "status": "checked",
    "checkedBy": "Dr Eslam Ahmed + Codex",
    "updated": "2026-05-16T09:02:20",
    "topicNote": "",
    "steps": [
      {
        "title": "The transformation y f (4",
        "body": "The transformation *y* = *f*(4*x*) represents a horizontal stretch by a scale factor of $\\\\frac{1}{4}$. The x-coordinate is multiplied by $\\\\frac{1}{4}$ while the y-coordinate stays the same.\n$$\\\\text{New minimum} = \\\\left(8 \\\\times \\\\frac{1}{4}, 1\\\\right) = (2, 1)$$"
      },
      {
        "title": "Answer",
        "body": "(2,1)\n\n**Step 1: Find the vertical shift *b*.** The standard *y* = cos (*x*) graph fluctuates between  − 1 and 1, centered at *y* = 0. The given graph fluctuates between a minimum of *y* = 1 and a maximum of *y* = 3. The center line is $\\\\frac{3 + 1}{2} = 2$. Therefore, *b* = 2.\n\n**Step 2: Find the frequency multiplier *a*.** The period of the given graph completes one full cycle between *x* = 0 and *x* = 90<sup>∘</sup> (it starts at 3, dips to 1, and returns to 3 at 90<sup>∘</sup>). The standard cosine period is 360<sup>∘</sup>.\n$$\\\\text{Period} = \\\\frac{360^\\\\circ}{a}$$\n$$90^\\\\circ = \\\\frac{360^\\\\circ}{a} \\\\implies a = 4$$"
      }
    ],
    "finalAnswer": "*a* = 4,  *b* = 2"
  },
